.TH HU_TLS_FD 3 "" "helios-unix" "Library functions"
.SH NAME
hu_tls_fd_attach, hu_tls_fd_get, hu_tls_fd_start, hu_tls_fd_handshake, hu_tls_fd_connect, hu_tls_fd_read, hu_tls_fd_write, hu_tls_fd_select, hu_tls_fd_close \- adapt descriptor-based applications to TLS
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <helios/unix/tls_fd.h>

int hu_tls_fd_attach(int fd, struct hu_tls *tls);
struct hu_tls *hu_tls_fd_get(int fd);
int hu_tls_fd_start(int fd, const char *peer, unsigned timeout_ms);
int hu_tls_fd_handshake(int fd, struct hu_tls_info *info);
int hu_tls_fd_connect(int fd, const char *peer, unsigned timeout_ms,
                       struct hu_tls_info *info);
int hu_tls_fd_read(int fd, void *buffer, unsigned size);
int hu_tls_fd_write(int fd, const void *buffer, unsigned size);
int hu_tls_fd_select(int nfds, fd_set *readfds, fd_set *writefds,
                      fd_set *exceptfds, struct timeval *timeout);
int hu_tls_fd_close(int fd);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
This task-local adapter associates descriptor numbers with TLS objects for
applications built around select. Route all read, write, select and close
operations through the adapter; unattached descriptors pass through to their
native operations. A registered fd is a key, not an encrypted OS descriptor.
Do not use dup, fdopen, send/recv, raw close, or pass it to another task.
.PP
hu_tls_fd_attach accepts a newly created, unattached TLS object and a connected
fd, taking both only on success. hu_tls_fd_get returns a borrowed object or
NULL for an unregistered descriptor. Do not destroy that borrowed object.
.PP
For the default provider, start accepts a fresh connected fd and begins
negotiation. It returns 0 if ready or pending, -1 on failure. Follow with
handshake, which fills a required info structure and returns 1 ready, 0
pending, or -1 failure. Use the object's HU_TLS_HANDSHAKE interest to wait.
connect performs this sequence synchronously, returning 0 on success or -1
on failure; info must be non-NULL.
.SS Failure ownership
Unlike attach, start takes ownership on entry. Setup failures close the fd;
handshake failures leave a registered failed object for adapter close.
After failure on a fresh fd, call hu_tls_fd_close only if hu_tls_fd_get returns
non-NULL. Do not blindly close the number: it may already have been released.
Starting an already attached descriptor fails with EALREADY and leaves that
existing connection intact. connect with NULL info fails before taking ownership.
.PP
Read/write use the TLS byte-count and EAGAIN conventions in hu_tls(3).
select translates physical TLS interests into requested application readiness,
including buffered plaintext and provider timers. Readiness means retry I/O;
it can still return EAGAIN. Native FD_SETSIZE limits apply. There is no TLS
urgent-data channel. Use this adapter from one event loop.
.PP
close removes the association before descriptor reuse. It gives established
sessions up to five seconds to drain shutdown, then releases the descriptor
even on error. Pending negotiation is cancelled. Never retry adapter close.
.SH EXAMPLE
This routine consumes a fresh connected fd on all paths and returns it ready
for adapter I/O, or -1 on error.
.EX
#include <helios/unix/tls_fd.h>
#include <errno.h>

int secure_fd(int fd, const char *peer)
{
    struct hu_tls_info info;
    int error;
    if (hu_tls_fd_connect(fd, peer, 10000, &info) < 0) {
        error = errno;
        if (hu_tls_fd_get(fd)) hu_tls_fd_close(fd);
        errno = error;
        return -1;
    }
    return fd;
}
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
hu_tls(3), helios-unix(7)
